1. Architecture Overview
1.1 Architecture Principles
The Inventory Management System (IMS) architecture is designed based on the following principles:
Cloud-First: Leverage cloud infrastructure for scalability, reliability, and reduced operational overhead
API-Driven Integration: All external system integrations use REST APIs or standard protocols
Separation of Concerns: Clear separation between presentation, business logic, and data layers
QuickBooks as Financial System of Record: Maintain QuickBooks authority for financial data while IMS manages operational inventory
Real-Time Data Sync: Minimize latency between systems to ensure data consistency
Modularity: Design for future extensibility and integration of new sales channels or warehouses
Security by Design: Implement security controls at every layer
High Availability: Ensure system uptime during business hours
1.2 Architecture Style
Selected Architecture: Layered Architecture with Microservices-Oriented Design Rationale:
Layered architecture provides clear separation of concerns and maintainability
Microservices-oriented design allows independent scaling of integration components
Hybrid approach balances complexity with flexibility for future growth
