3. Physical / Deployment Architecture
3.1 Deployment Model
Recommended: Cloud-based SaaS or PaaS deployment Cloud Provider Options:
AWS (Amazon Web Services): Mature ecosystem, strong integration options
Microsoft Azure: Excellent QuickBooks and Office 365 integration
Google Cloud Platform (GCP): Cost-effective, strong analytics capabilities
Rationale for Cloud:
Scalability: Handle growth without infrastructure investment
High Availability: Built-in redundancy and failover
Reduced Operational Overhead: Managed services reduce IT burden
Security: Enterprise-grade security controls
Geographic Distribution: Multi-region deployment for data residency (US/Canada)
3.2 Infrastructure Components
3.3.1 Web / Application Tier
Technology: AWS ECS (Elastic Container Service) or EKS (Kubernetes) Configuration:
Containerization: Docker containers for portability
Orchestration: Kubernetes (EKS) or AWS ECS for container management
Auto-Scaling: Horizontal scaling based on CPU/memory utilization
Load Balancing: Application Load Balancer (ALB) for traffic distribution
Instances: Minimum 2 instances for high availability, scale up to 10+
3.3.2 Database Tier
Technology: AWS RDS PostgreSQL (Relational Database Service) Configuration:
Multi-AZ Deployment: Primary and standby instances for high availability
Instance Type: db.m5.large (2 vCPU, 8 GB RAM) - adjust based on load
Storage: 100 GB SSD (General Purpose), auto-scaling up to 1 TB
Backup: Automated daily backups, 7-day retention
Read Replicas: Optional read replicas for reporting queries (future)
3.3.3 Caching Layer
Technology: AWS ElastiCache (Redis) Purpose:
Session storage
Application-level caching (frequently accessed data)
Real-time dashboard data
Configuration:
Instance Type: cache.m5.large
Replication: Multi-AZ for high availability
3.3.4 File Storage
Technology: AWS S3 (Simple Storage Service) Purpose:
Report exports (Excel, PDF)
System logs
Backup storage
Configuration:
Bucket Policy: Private with role-based access
Versioning: Enabled for critical files
Lifecycle Policy: Transition to Glacier for long-term archival
3.3.5 Background Job Processing
Technology: AWS Lambda or AWS Batch Use Cases:
Daily forecasting job
Daily reconciliation job
Scheduled report generation
Alert processing
3.3.6 Monitoring & Logging
Technology: AWS CloudWatch, Datadog, or New Relic Metrics Monitored:
Application performance (response time, error rate)
Infrastructure health (CPU, memory, disk)
Database performance (query time, connections)
Integration health (API call success rate)
4. Integration Architecture
4.1 Integration Patterns
4.1.1 API-Based Integration (RESTful)
Used For: QuickBooks, Shopify, Amazon, Faire, Extensiv & Diamond WMS (if API available) Characteristics:
Synchronous request-response
JSON payload format
OAuth 2.0 or API key authentication
Rate limiting and throttling
Retry logic for transient failures
4.1.2 Webhook / Event-Driven Integration
Used For: Sales channel order notifications, inventory updates Characteristics:
Asynchronous push notifications
IMS exposes webhook endpoints
External systems push events to IMS
Event queuing for processing (AWS SQS)
4.1.3 File-Based Integration (Fallback)
Used For: Systems without APIs (e.g., legacy Diamond WMS if needed) Characteristics:
Scheduled file transfer (SFTP, S3)
CSV or XML file format
Batch processing (nightly or periodic)
Manual reconciliation if real-time unavailable
4.2 Integration Architecture Diagram

4.3 Integration Details by System
4.3.1 QuickBooks Online Integration
Integration Method: REST API via OAuth 2.0
Data Flow:
		IMS → QuickBooks:
Inventory quantities (real-time sync)
Purchase Orders
Inventory adjustments (Journal Entries)
Transfer Journal Entries
		QuickBooks → IMS:
Product/SKU master data
Supplier information
Inventory valuation data
Sync Frequency: Real-time or <5 minutes batch API Endpoints Used:
/item - Inventory items
/purchaseorder - Purchase orders
/journalentry - Adjustments and transfers
/account - Chart of accounts
Challenges & Mitigations:
		Rate Limits: QuickBooks API has strict rate limits
		Mitigation: Implement intelligent batching, caching, and throttling
		Data Model Differences: QuickBooks inventory model may differ from IMS
		Mitigation: Mapping layer to translate between models
		Authentication: OAuth tokens expire
		Mitigation: Automatic token refresh mechanism
4.3.2 Extensiv WMS Integration (Canada)
Integration Method: REST API (assuming API available) Data Flow:
		IMS → Extensiv:
Customer orders for fulfillment
Expected receipts (POs)
		Transfer instructions
		Extensiv → IMS:
Inventory levels (real-time)
Order fulfillment confirmations
Receipt confirmations
Sync Frequency: Real-time via webhooks (preferred) or <5 minutes polling
Assumption: Extensiv provides REST API. If not, escalate for custom integration.
4.3.3 Diamond Fulfillment WMS Integration (USA)
Integration Method: Custom API or File-Based (TBD based on Diamond's capabilities) Data Flow:
		IMS → Diamond:
Customer orders
Expected receipts
		Diamond → IMS:
Inventory levels
Fulfillment confirmations
Receipt confirmations
Challenges:
Current setup uses custom "inventory report website" by Aaron
May lack standard API
Mitigation:
Option 1: Work with Diamond to provide API access
Option 2: Custom integration with Diamond's system
Option 3: File-based integration (SFTP or S3) as fallback
4.3.4 Shopify Integration
Integration Method: Shopify REST API & GraphQL API
Data Flow:
		IMS → Shopify:
		Inventory level updates
		Shopify → IMS:
Customer orders (via webhook)
Product catalog sync
Sync Frequency: Real-time via webhooks API Endpoints Used:
/inventory_levels - Update inventory
/orders - Receive orders
Webhooks: orders/create, inventory_levels/update
4.3.5 Amazon (MWS / SP-API) Integration
Integration Method: Amazon Marketplace Web Service (MWS) or Selling Partner API (SP-API) Data Flow:
		IMS → Amazon:
Inventory level updates
Shipment confirmations
		Amazon → IMS:
Customer orders
Inventory levels at FBA warehouses
Sync Frequency: Polling every 5-15 minutes (Amazon rate limits) Challenges:
Amazon API has strict rate limits
Complex API with steep learning curve
Different APIs for US and Canada
Mitigation:
Use Amazon SP-API (newer, more reliable)
Implement intelligent polling and caching
Leverage Amazon webhooks where available
4.3.6 Faire Integration
Integration Method: Faire API (REST) Data Flow:
		IMS → Faire:
		Inventory level updates
		Faire → IMS:
		Customer orders
Sync Frequency: Real-time via webhooks
4.4 Integration Error Handling Strategy
4.4.1 Retry Logic
Transient Errors: Retry up to 3 times with exponential backoff
Permanent Errors: Log and alert for manual intervention
4.4.2 Error Queue
Failed integration requests stored in queue (AWS SQS)
Manual retry or resolution by administrator
4.4.3 Integration Health Dashboard
Display integration status for each system (green/yellow/red)
Show last successful sync time
Alert on sync failures
4.4.4 Circuit Breaker Pattern
Temporarily disable integration if external system is down
Prevent cascading failures
Automatic retry after cooldown period

