8. Scalability & Performance Considerations
8.1 Horizontal Scalability
Strategy: Auto-scaling application tier based on load Implementation:
Containerized application (Docker)
Orchestration via Kubernetes or AWS ECS
Auto-scaling policies based on CPU/memory utilization
Load balancer distributes traffic across instances
Benefits:
Handle traffic spikes during promotions or seasonal peaks
		Cost-effective: Scale down during low-traffic periods
8.2 Database Optimization Strategies:
Indexing: Index frequently queried columns (ProductID, WarehouseID, SKU, dates)
Query Optimization: Use EXPLAIN to analyze and optimize slow queries
Connection Pooling: Limit database connections to prevent overload
Read Replicas: Offload reporting and analytics queries to read replicas
Caching: Cache frequently accessed data (product master, warehouse list) in Redis
8.3 Caching Strategy Levels of Caching:
Application Cache (Redis):
Product master data
Warehouse locations
User sessions
Dashboard summary data
Database Query Cache:
Enable query caching for repeated queries
Cache expiration: 5-15 minutes
CDN (CloudFront):
Cache static assets (CSS, JS, images)
Reduce latency for global users
8.4 Asynchronous Processing Use Cases:
Daily forecasting job
Daily reconciliation job
Bulk data imports
Report generation
Implementation:
Background job queue (AWS Lambda, AWS Batch, or Celery)
Scheduled jobs via cron or AWS EventBridge
Event-driven processing for real-time alerts
Benefits:
Offload heavy processing from main application
Improve user experience (no waiting for long tasks)
8.5 API Rate Limiting & Throttling Purpose: Prevent abuse and ensure fair usage Implementation:
Rate limit: 100 requests/minute per user
Throttle excessive requests with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests)
API key-based rate limiting for external integrations



